---
/**
 * Module I.1, Week 0, from the approved outline of 16 Oct 2026.
 * FLAG: the outline dates the writing-down of the epics to the 8th–7th
 * century BC and marks that date as debated. The prose says so.
 * FLAG: “about 1600–1200 BC” and “around 1200 BC” are the outline’s dates,
 * not a new chronology. No date is given for a historical Trojan War.
 * The Lion Gate and the mask’s date are the drafted syllabus.
 * Linear B is described only as the outline describes it: palace records
 * of a palace economy. No tablet is quoted.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import LessonChrome from "../../components/LessonChrome.astro";
import Reader from "../../components/Reader.astro";
import Introspection from "../../components/Introspection.astro";
import References from "../../components/References.astro";
import plate from "../../assets/lessons/flaxman-iliad-1.jpg";
import book1 from "../../data/iliad-book1.json";
import { moduleExcerpts } from "../../data/excerpts";
const proem = moduleExcerpts["the-heroic-code"][0];
---

<Base
  title="The World of Homer"
  description="The history lesson that opens Module 1: Mycenaean palaces, the collapse, oral poetry, and why the Iliad is the first textbook of this course."
>
  <LessonChrome slug="the-world-of-homer">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>The World of Homer</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Today we add the world this honor stands in. The prologue is the ground
        under it: farming, Crete, and the palaces. The poem itself begins in the
        next lesson.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="learn" aria-labelledby="will-learn">
      <h2 id="will-learn">What you’ll learn</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Mycenae and Pylos were palace kingdoms. Linear B is the writing of a palace economy, and that writing was lost.</li>
        <li>Around 1200 BC those kingdoms collapsed. The centuries after were poorer, with fewer settlements. Historians call this the Dark Age.</li>
        <li>The epics came out of oral poetry: bards composing in performance, with formulas and epithets. When they were written down is debated.</li>
        <li>The world the poems describe is not the same as the world of the poet’s audience. Greeks treated Homer as a shared textbook for centuries. This course begins there.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="lesson-body">
      <p class="scene-today">Today we add the world this honor stands in to the foundation.</p>
      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>Let’s begin before the quarrel</h3>
      <p>
        The next lesson will open the <em>Iliad</em> on a quarrel in a camp. Before
        you hear that quarrel, you need the ground it stands on. This course builds
        one man, layer by layer, as Greek society grows more complex. The first
        society is the simplest in the sequence: chieftains, war bands, and
        personal loyalty. Honor, the virtue of that society, is the first layer.
        It has nothing under it but the prologue’s ground and this history.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>Palace kingdoms, then a collapse</h3>
      <p>
        This layer belongs to the Bronze Age and at Mycenae, about
        1600–1200 BC. Mycenae and Pylos are the palace kingdoms you should be able
        to name. Their records are in Linear B, a script that kept the accounts of
        a palace economy. Two objects are worth knowing: the Lion Gate at Mycenae, in
        the UNESCO listing “Archaeological Sites of Mycenae and Tiryns,” and to
        the gold mask Schliemann called Agamemnon’s, from Grave Circle A, about
        1550–1500 BC. The name is Schliemann’s. The mask is centuries older than
        the Trojan War as later Greeks told it. The poem is not a report of that
        mask.
      </p>
      <p>
        Around 1200 BC the palace kingdoms collapsed. What followed, on the
        outline’s account, was centuries of fewer and poorer settlements. That
        stretch is the Dark Age of this course. Linear B, the palace writing, was
        lost with the palaces. A technology of memory had to be found again, and
        it was not the same writing.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>A poem kept by the voice</h3>
      <p>
        The epics came out of oral poetry. Bards composed in performance. They had
        formulas and epithets, set phrases a singer can reuse, so a long story can
        be held without a book. The Homeric question, in plain terms, is how that
        singing became the poems we have: one poet, or many, and how performance
        relates to a written text. The epics were probably written down in
        the 8th–7th century BC, but that date is debated.
        Treat the century as unsettled. Treat the fact of oral composition as the
        point.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hold the gap. The poems describe an older world, of palaces and
        chieftains, than the world of the poet’s later audience. When you read
        Agamemnon’s camp, you are not looking at a day in the audience’s own
        city. You are looking at a world the song remembered. That is why a prize,
        a guest-gift, and a name in the song matter so much in the lessons that
        follow. They are how worth was stored when the palace writing was gone.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>Why this is the first textbook</h3>
      <p>
        Later Greeks treated Homer as their shared textbook for centuries. This
        course takes that use as its reason for beginning here. A professional
        today inherits practices, laws, and buildings whose reasons are older than
        the office. The first reason in this sequence is a public name: what a man
        is worth in other men’s eyes, and what a song can keep after he is dead.
        The next lesson gives that idea its Greek names, <em>timē</em> and
        <em>kleos</em>, inside Book 1. Today you only need the ground. The
        invention this module is here to teach is the hexameter line and the
        formula, a technology of memory after writing had been lost.
      </p>
      <p>
        The passage below is the opening of the poem, in Samuel Butler’s English
        of 1898, with the Greek of the same lines. It is the door, not yet the
        quarrel.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Reader
      heading="The text: the opening of the Iliad"
      note="Samuel Butler, 1898, the first paragraph of Book 1. The Greek is Iliad 1.1–7 from the Perseus Digital Library. The rest of Book 1 is the next lesson."
      sourceHref={book1.source}
      paragraphs={[book1.paragraphs[0]]}
      greek={proem.greek}
      greekCaption="Iliad 1.1–7. Greek text, Perseus Digital Library. Butler’s English follows."
    />

    <figure class="figure lesson-figure">
      <Image
        src={plate}
        alt="Line engraving: Achilles seated by the sea with Thetis before him, while a woman is led away along the shore."
        width={1600}
        height={953}
      />
      <figcaption>
        The camp the poem will teach, not a view of the Lion Gate. Achilles at the
        shore, and Thetis before him. Engraving by Tommaso Piroli, 1795, after
        John Flaxman’s drawing of 1793. Wikimedia Commons identifies the plate as
        an illustration to <em>Iliad</em> Book 1, lines 354–358. Public domain.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="word-day" aria-labelledby="word-day">
      <h2 id="word-day">Word of the day</h2>
      <p class="word-greek" lang="grc">κλέος</p>
      <p>
        <em>kleos</em>, glory that outlives you. That is this course’s definition
        for the layer, not a line quoted from a lexicon. The song is the thing
        that stores it. The first word of the poem, in the Greek above, is
        μῆνιν, the anger. You will learn that word properly in the next lesson.
        For today, say <em>kleos</em> once, and remember what the voice was for.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Introspection
      slug="the-world-of-homer"
      prompts={[
        "When the palaces fell, Linear B was lost, and what survived was carried by memory and song. What does your own work, family or field still keep by memory and reputation once the official record is gone?",
        "Homer’s poems were composed in performance, with formulas passed from singer to singer. What do you know only because someone taught it to you aloud, and who will you hand it on to?",
        "Later Greeks used Homer as a shared textbook for centuries. What stories does your household or workplace share that teach what is worth admiring? Are they the right ones?",
        "The poem keeps a picture of worth from a world older than its audience. Which standards from an older generation in your life do you still measure yourself by, and which have you set aside?",
      ]}
    />

    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
      <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>There is no layer of the man under this one. The prologue is the ground: palaces that rose and fell. This lesson is the world of chieftains and war bands, remembered after that fall.</li>
        <li>Linear B stored a palace economy and was lost. Formula and hexameter stored a song. That is the invention this module is carrying.</li>
        <li>The poems describe an older world than the world of their audience. Read the camp as memory, not as a diary of the poet’s city.</li>
        <li>Homer is the first textbook of the course because later Greeks used him as one. The next lesson opens the book on honor itself.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="summary">
        You now have the ground: palaces, a collapse, a Dark Age, and a poem kept
        by the voice. Honor, in the next lesson, is what that poem thinks a man
        is worth.
      </p>
    </section>

    <aside class="go-deeper">
      <h2>Go deeper, if you want</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing here is required. You can look up the Lion Gate and, for the
        next lesson, the mask from Grave Circle A. A grammar book can wait. The
        next lesson has Book 1 in full.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <References slug="the-world-of-homer" />
  </LessonChrome>
</Base>

